From: Omarchy Shield contributors
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: tegra: encode Darcy VBUS open-drain GPIO flags

Both USB VBUS enable nodes already declare gpio-open-drain, but the
modern gpiolib legacy-property quirk only matches reg-fixed-voltage,
not their regulator-fixed compatible. Encode the existing electrical
intent in the GPIO specifier itself. Keep the original pins, polarity,
voltages and legacy property unchanged.

A bounded Darcy A04 RAM test with these two flag cells verified the live
DT and raw Tegra CNF/OE/OUT/IN bits against working recovery. The drive
enable is released high and the PC enable remains driven low. This does
not resolve external-drive enumeration or qualify the physical 5V rail.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/nvidia/tegra210-p2894.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/nvidia/tegra210-p2894.dtsi
@@ -2034,7 +2034,7 @@
 		regulator-min-microvolt = <5000000>;
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <5000000>;
 
-		gpio = <&gpio TEGRA_GPIO(CC, 5) GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		gpio = <&gpio TEGRA_GPIO(CC, 5) (GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH | GPIO_OPEN_DRAIN)>;
 		enable-active-high;
 		gpio-open-drain;
 	};
@@ -2045,7 +2045,7 @@
 		regulator-min-microvolt = <5000000>;
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <5000000>;
 
-		gpio = <&gpio TEGRA_GPIO(CC, 4) GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		gpio = <&gpio TEGRA_GPIO(CC, 4) (GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH | GPIO_OPEN_DRAIN)>;
 		enable-active-high;
 		gpio-open-drain;
 	};
